\f1\i0 ,\'94 he said and paused.\
\'93If you hadn\'92t done it, we wouldn\'92t have to vote!\'94 the studio audience chorused back, then erupted in applause and cheers.\
\'93Yes, indeed,\'94 Alexander said. \'93Let\'92s not keep out special guest waiting.\'94\
\'93Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!\'94 the audience chanted.\
Alexander chuckled and waved down the noise. \'93Airborne ladies and gentleman, please join me in reviling Cloudborne Hamish Hammersmith.\'94\
The audience erupted as two giant body-builder types\'97their skins glistening with oil\'97dragged an emaciated sod before Alexander\'92s raised dias, and dumped him with rehearsed ceremony on the bare studio floorboards.\
\'93You have the wrong man,\'94 the sod said, as instructed by his production handler. \'93I haven\'92t done anything.\'94 The audience laughed at his pathetic petition, as Alexander hoped everyone streaming at home would laugh.\
\'93Play the package,\'94 Alexander said to camera, exactly as he had hundreds of times before. He hated the money production spent on the multiple holographic sequences of \'91evidence\'92 they played. But their design schtick was an integral part of the show: grainy, high-contrast imaging with most of the colour leached out, all calculated to cast the \'91special guest\'92 as loathsome and reprehensible. Each ended with a brand-conscious dissolve of a Hammersmith\'92s magnified, evil face.\
\'93Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!\'94 the audience resumed. \
In the early days of the show, Alexender begrudgingly conceded the evidence packages were a major attractant of eyeballs to the show, eclipsed only by his talent as celebrity anchor. Years on, he resented the production costs all the more.\
The 
\fs22 QUIET PLEASE 
\fs26 sign above the studio floor illuminated. The audience settled to murmurs.\
\'93What have you to say now, Hamish Hammersmith?\'94 Alexander read from the autocue. The next part of the show\'97where he challenged the special guest, and tricked them into inconsistent testimony\'97passed with Alexander on autopilot. He\'92d given so many such performances, they were all a blur to him. While they were passing, it wasn\'92t uncommon for him to be reminiscing, say on a meal he\'92d once had, perhaps a spectacular mac and cheese.\
\'93It\'92s time for the verdict!\'94 Alexander boomed, his engagement with proceedings reignited.\
\'93If you hadn\'92t done it, we wouldn\'92t have to vote!\'94 The audience knew the formula.\
Votes poured in. After time had expired, Alexander announced, \'93The votes are in, my friends.\'94 The audience hushed. \'93We received 2.17 million votes. There might be some folks voting more than once, but here on 
