\f1\fs26 The Wheel of Death had been rigged to stop at 
\fs22 TREBUCHET SLAP
\fs26 \'97Alexander\'92s favorite episode climax, both for its effect on ratings and for the production intricacies it required to pull off. While all execution scenes were, by necessity, one-take wonders\'97there could be no do-overs\'97the trebuchet slap, in particular, required perfect timing and execution (if you\'92ll pardon the pun). 
\f2\i Random Camera\'92s
\f1\i0  trebuchet was the typical medieval sturdy, wooden siege engine\'97an 
\f2\i A
\f1\i0 -frame set upon a wheeled platform\'97complete with hulking iron brackets, giant round head nails, ropes and pulleys. A thick beam about 6 metres long was axled at the frame\'92s apex. At one end of the beam hung a large counter weight; at the other, a lengthy sling and pouch where the convict would be strapped in. The counter-weight would be winched up and ratcheted in place. When the executioner (a swimsuit model, as mandated by every focus group ever) released the ratchet, the counter-weight would fall, and over would sling convict, cradle and all. The magic of 
\f2\i Random Camera\'92s 
\f1\i0 corrupted trebuchet was, it would not hurl its payload many metres in the air, such as when laying siege to a castle. No, this trebuchet, by perversion of its design, hurled its hapless passenger straight into the ground within fractured reach of its splintery wheels.\
Convict pancake anyone?\
Yes, the trebuchet slap was Alexander\'92s favorite. It was difficult to perfect. In the early days, if a convict appeared to survive the engine, they were pardoned. But freeing convicts lodged awkwardly in the audience\'92s craw, and eventually Marketing demanded a trebuchet slam survivor be subject to a second spin of the wheel, which was then usually rigged to CLUBBING. As much as his fans loved a double event, Alexander preferred a successful first execution. He was a professional.\
